<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Range interquartile range and quartile deviation</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The numbers of M-Pesa transactions at an agent in Nyeri on \({@n@}\) days were (not in order)</p><p style="text-align:center;">{@gstr@}</p><p>(a) Find the range.</p><div>Range \(=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Find the interquartile range.</p><div>IQR \(=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><p>(c) Find the quartile deviation. Give an exact value.</p><div>QD \(=\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Put the data in order first: {@sstr@}.</p><p>(a) The range is the largest value minus the smallest: \({@s[n]@} - {@s[1]@} = {@rg@}\).</p><p>(b) With \(n = {@n@}\), \(Q_1\) is at position \(({@n@} + 1)/4 = {@p1@}\) and \(Q_3\) at \(3({@n@} + 1)/4 = {@p3@}\), so \(Q_1 = {@q1@}\) and \(Q_3 = {@q3@}\). The interquartile range, the spread of the middle half of the data, is \({@q3@} - {@q1@} = {@iqr@}\).</p><p>(c) The quartile deviation (semi-interquartile range) is half the IQR: \({@iqr@} \div 2 = {@exd(qd)@}\).</p><p>The range uses only the two extreme days, so one unusual day changes it. The IQR and quartile deviation use the middle half and are not affected by extremes.</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>3</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026063000</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[/* exd: shortest exact decimal display of a terminating value (up to 3 d.p.) */
exd(v) := if integerp(v) then v else if integerp(10*v) then dispdp(float(v),1) else if integerp(100*v) then dispdp(float(v),2) else dispdp(float(v),3);
/* Design: quartiles and IQR of a list, with range and QD. n is 11, 15 or 19, so the quartile positions are whole numbers: the skill here is the spread, not interpolation. Source: adapted from SMP AS/A2 Statistics 1, chapter 1, Exercise B question 2. */
dl: [[68,31,49,58,21,63,79,47,57,48,38],[20,47,34,67,52,30,63,73,41,21,23,78,38,77,75],[69,37,27,31,75,78,76,79,25,41,33,46,21,50,77,23,68,74,28],[73,71,22,48,58,36,31,78,76,44,41],[68,44,78,69,21,51,37,24,55,58,57,75,30,63,46],[50,30,56,78,58,47,23,20,61,60,73,68,59,22,75,74,41,70,69],[48,36,42,56,38,30,26,21,60,61,45],[39,41,73,42,29,70,61,43,31,50,44,45,62,53,69],[72,31,69,53,46,28,76,51,62,36,58,49,66,25,61,44,74,48,26],[66,22,25,46,44,73,51,61,32,60,34]];
ix: 1+rand(10);
g: dl[ix];
n: length(g);
s: sort(g);
p1: (n+1)/4;
p3: 3*(n+1)/4;
q1: s[p1];
q3: s[p3];
rg: s[n]-s[1];
iqr: q3-q1;
qd: iqr/2;
gstr: simplode(map(string,g),", ");
sstr: simplode(map(string,s),", ");
/* diagnostics */
rgu: last(g)-first(g);
iqru: g[p3]-g[p1];
qdavg: (q1+q3)/2;
/* test values */
rgn: -rg; iqrn: -iqr; qdn: -qd-1;
iqro: iqr+2; qdo: (iqr+2)/2;]]></text>
  </questionvariables>
